import{_ as t,P as n,d as a,e as s,y as o,q as r,f as l}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const y=JSON.parse(`{"title":"D.12 Religion, Comparisons Across Civilizations, and Relational Knowledge","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"D.12 Religion, Comparisons Across Civilizations, and Relational Knowledge","xkChars":0,"xkWords":504,"xkLessons":{"D.12":{"title":"D.12 Religion, Comparative Civilizations, and Relational Knowledge","lead":"Understanding a body of knowledge does not automatically confer power to disclose it, reuse it, or represent its source community. Comparing religions, civilizations, and relational knowledge should begin by identifying specific texts, institutions, practices, and tasks, respecting protection boundaries while retaining tests of concrete effects and safety.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 5158–5173; source anchor section-d-12; brief quotation translated from Chinese: “Knowledge being locatable, publicly disclosable, permitted for reuse, and authorized to represent its source community are different qualifications.” An exact source excerpt is separately attached in source-slice.md; explanation and source text remain separate.","Greek philosophy, Judaism and Christianity, Islamic thought, South Asian traditions, cross-cultural Buddhism, African thought, Indigenous knowledge, Latin American liberation thought, and Southeast Asian and Pacific experience are not civilizational individuals with unified wills. Writing that a civilization wants something or must behave a certain way conflates internal heterogeneity, historical change, and political power into a single subject. Specify the text, institution, whose practice, and period being discussed.","Textual doctrines, institutional rules, personal beliefs, state power, reform movements, and lived practices each require their own material. A doctrinal proposition does not automatically describe how all adherents live; state action does not represent every religious member's wishes; and a reform text does not guarantee comprehensive institutional change. Local institutional improvements and remaining exclusions can coexist; comparison cannot be written as an overall ranking of civilizations.","Relational knowledge may include detailed environmental observations, skills, and arrangements of responsibility. A skill genuinely working for a particular task does not require researchers first to exhaust its mechanisms. Yet a successful skill cannot universally validate every accompanying cosmological explanation. Check task performance, conditions of formation and transmission, mechanism explanations, and normative significance separately, rather than settling every claim through one success.","Religious and philosophical arguments can offer independent understanding and normative value without all being reduced to empirical predictions. At the same time, moral, political, and empirical claims remain open to criticism under their respective conditions: empirical effects require corresponding material; normative claims require explicit reasons and affected positions; the value of understanding can be discussed in terms of concepts and meanings in life. Respect for a tradition neither exempts every specific claim from examination nor requires translation into an external model before it counts as knowledge.","Four kinds of standing must be stated separately: whether knowledge can be located, whether material may be disclosed publicly, whether a particular use is permitted, and whether someone is authorized to represent the source community. Knowing a location or understanding terminology resolves only a limited epistemic question; protective nondisclosure does not establish that knowledge is absent. An outside researcher cannot derive authority to disseminate sensitive material or decide for a community from having learned a concept.","The sufficiency of translation depends on the task. For communicating navigational directions, stable correspondences among certain terms may support cooperation on that task. Relational responsibilities, rituals, and meanings in life may still contain aspects that cannot align directly through the same vocabulary. This is neither total incomprehensibility nor complete equivalence. Specify which distinctions translation retains and which uses require further explanation and confirmation.","The source's navigation example allows both sides to retain bounded achievements and limits. Local practice may reliably accomplish a class of tasks, while an outside model explains some trajectories, allowing reciprocal error checks. Explicit equations do not confer authority over life decisions, and cultural identity does not exempt specific safety or performance claims from checking. This is a teaching assumption, not a performance assessment of or authorization concerning any real navigating community.","Comparability over time should likewise be judged for specific items and uses. Changing an item's wording or survey mode may prevent directly joining it to an older series. If other items have adequate bridging evidence, their trends do not all become invalid as a result. Conversely, an unchanged survey name does not guarantee identical meanings or selection into responses for every question. Local differences should restrict conclusions dependent on them, rather than automatically erase all material.","Follow-up after the same random assignment adds temporal information, not independent trials. Outcomes at multiple times for the same participants may be correlated; they cannot be counted as independent studies or used to arbitrarily increase certainty. Nor is significance in one period and nonsignificance in another a test of differences between periods. Effect changes must be compared directly, considering standard errors, correlation, and design, rather than comparing two labels.","Bounded skills and service improvements can be established while authority over personal agendas, refusal, and remedies still requires separate assessment. Helping improve one task does not automatically allow the provider to decide every aspect of life. Service recipients' lack of governance authority likewise does not require denying the bounded help received. Preserve parallel conclusions rather than upgrading one improvement into resolution of every rights issue.","The thirteen entry points are, in order: Plato, Aristotle, and the Stoics; Jewish and Christian traditions; Islamic thought and communities; Indian Buddhism, Hinduism, and Jainism; Buddhism across cultures; secularization, reform, and revival; Africa and Ubuntu; Indigenous relational knowledge; Latin American liberation and decolonization; civilizational translation and nonalignment; caste, hierarchy, and reform in South Asia; autonomous history in Southeast Asia; and Pacific navigation and relational knowledge. An entry point does not make each tradition homogeneous. The strongest objection is that respecting boundaries could stop examination. The response is that the need to examine does not automatically create standing to disclose or represent, while specific claims obtained with permission remain open to criticism by appropriate standards."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Locating knowledge","leftText":"Being able to identify where material or knowledge is situated.","rightTitle":"Standing to disclose, reuse, and represent","rightText":"Each requires its own grounds and permission; understanding does not automatically confer them."},{"leftTitle":"A skill's bounded effectiveness","leftText":"Performance on a specified task can be supported.","rightTitle":"All explanations are true","rightText":"Accompanying mechanisms or cosmologies still require their respective material and arguments."},{"leftTitle":"Translation sufficient for a task","leftText":"Certain distinctions suffice for the current cooperation.","rightTitle":"Complete equivalence of meaning","rightText":"Other uses, such as relational responsibility, may still contain aspects that do not align."},{"leftTitle":"Multiple follow-ups within one trial","leftText":"Add temporal and trajectory information.","rightTitle":"Multiple independent trials","rightText":"Require independent designs and information structures; a count label cannot create them."},{"leftTitle":"Significant in one period, nonsignificant in another","leftText":"Each estimate is judged against its own uncertainty.","rightTitle":"A significant difference between period effects","rightText":"Requires directly testing the difference and accounting for correlation and design."}],"boundaries":["Do not treat civilizations, religions, or cultural groups as person-like agents with a single will.","Do not disclose sensitive community knowledge or derive authorization to reuse or represent from understanding public terminology.","Protecting culture and checking specific safety and performance can proceed together; identity gives no exemption from examination, and an explicit model does not confer decision authority.","The navigation examples and cross-period numbers are teaching constructions; no actual navigation, survey, or trial was conducted.","The real label case establishes only the publisher's explanation of rules; it does not claim adoption by all communities or effective protection already achieved."],"questions":["Why does a skill's effectiveness not require first identifying every mechanism?","Why cannot the four kinds of knowledge-related standing be merged?","What do multiple follow-ups add, and what do they not add?","Can partial reforms and remaining exclusions both be established?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-12/figures/section-d-12.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"An actual protocol design: Local Contexts TK Labels","href":"https://localcontexts.org/labels/traditional-knowledge-labels/"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"An actual protocol design: Local Contexts TK Labels","text":"Local Contexts' official TK Labels explanation separates provenance, access protocols, and permissions for use. Permission Labels explicitly require direct contact with the relevant cultural authority for other uses. The TK Community Use Only page further explains that some material normally circulates only within a family, clan, or community and that communities customize the labels. This supports separating findable material from authorized reuse; it does not establish that any reader has actual permission.","sourceLabel":"Official protocol explanation from the publisher Local Contexts","sourceUrl":"https://localcontexts.org/labels/traditional-knowledge-labels/","sourceLocator":"Paragraphs 2–3 of the TK Labels introduction; explanations of the Provenance/Protocol/Permission categories, especially Permission Labels lines 53–54; also Why use, Template Text, and How to use on TK Community Use Only","boundary":"Verifies the existence of official rules and templates, without claiming successful implementation by a particular community, using restricted material, or deciding who is authorized to represent a group.","href":"https://localcontexts.org/labels/traditional-knowledge-labels/"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"D.12","start_line":4884,"end_line":4899,"startLine":4884,"endLine":4899}],"outputId":"section-d-12","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","argumentSteps":["Specify the particular objects, periods, and speaking positions for texts, institutions, beliefs, states, reforms, and practices.","Evaluate skill outcomes, mechanism explanations, cosmologies, and normative understanding separately, without demanding prior knowledge of every mechanism or permitting unlimited expansion.","Separately check standing to locate, disclose, reuse, and represent; do not record protective nondisclosure as nonexistence.","Explain which tasks translation suffices for and where alignment remains incomplete; check errors in both directions without conferring control over lives.","Check bridging item by item across periods; do not count follow-ups in one trial as independent trials; differences in significance require a direct test of the effect difference."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Expanding the source's teaching example: mutual checks between skills and models","text":"Suppose a hypothetical community's method reliably completes a class of navigation tasks, while an outside model explains some trajectories. Within permitted bounds, both sides can compare errors, task conditions, and their respective limits. Clear model equations cannot transfer authority over life decisions to outsiders, and traditional identity cannot justify rejecting all specific safety checks.","assumptions":"Continues the source's hypothetical example; not actual navigation history or a safety assessment.","boundary":"Offers no real-world navigational advice and does not infer the truth of all knowledge or beliefs of any group."},{"title":"Teaching construction: one changed item does not cancel every trend","text":"Suppose survey item A changes from satisfaction to perceived safety, while item B retains the same definition and has suitable bridging evidence. A's values from two periods cannot be joined directly into one trend; B can still be analyzed to the extent supported by its bridging. Restrict conclusions dependent on A locally, rather than declaring every cross-period question in the entire survey meaningless.","assumptions":"The questions and bridging conditions are stipulated, not actual survey findings.","boundary":"Whether actual bridging suffices requires checking wording, mode, samples, and applicable targets, not merely identical item names."},{"title":"Mathematical teaching construction: significant versus nonsignificant does not mean different effects","text":"Suppose effect estimates in two periods are both 0.20, with normal-approximation standard errors of 0.09 and 0.12. Their respective z values are about 2.22 and 1.67. Under the two-sided 5% threshold of 1.96, one is significant and the other is not, but the difference in point estimates is 0. Provided the standard error of the difference is positive and appropriately estimated, the difference has z=0; the two labels cannot establish that the effect changed.","assumptions":"The values and normal tests are teaching models; no statistics from an actual study were calculated.","boundary":"For actual correlated follow-ups, the standard error of the difference requires design information such as covariance; this example does not assume that real follow-ups are independent."},{"title":"Teaching construction: three follow-ups still constitute one randomized trial","text":"Suppose one hypothetical random assignment is followed up at months 1, 6, and 12. Temporal changes can be studied, but the three reports cannot be counted as three independent randomized trials; attrition and measurement changes must also be checked at each period. Service improvement and participants' ability to refuse, obtain remedies, or lead the agenda require separate judgments.","assumptions":"The months and study are constructed.","boundary":"No intervention effect is actually estimated; the added information from follow-up is not treated as zero. Only erroneous independent counting is rejected."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"This knowledge is not public, so it does not exist and contains nothing worth understanding.","effect":"Protective nondisclosure differs from nonexistence; standing to disclose requires independent checking.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"I translated the terms accurately, so I may publish sensitive content and represent the group.","effect":"Understanding, disclosure, reuse, and representation are not the same standing.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"The first period is significant and the second is not, so their effects differ significantly.","effect":"The effect difference must be tested directly; identical point estimates with different standard errors provide a counterexample.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why does a skill's effectiveness not require first identifying every mechanism?","answer":"Performance on a bounded task can receive direct support, but does not validate every accompanying mechanism or cosmological explanation.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why cannot the four kinds of knowledge-related standing be merged?","answer":"Locating and understanding, public dissemination, specific reuse, and group representation involve different permissions and affected people.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"What do multiple follow-ups add, and what do they not add?","answer":"They add temporal and trajectory information without automatically increasing the number of independent randomized trials.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Can partial reforms and remaining exclusions both be established?","answer":"Yes. Each has specific objects and evidence; there is no need to give an entire civilization an overall ranking of winners and losers.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-12/figures/section-d-12.png","alt":"Texts / institutions / beliefs, states / reforms / practices do not combine into a civilizational personality; skills may be boundedly effective while mechanisms remain partly unknown, without making every cosmology true; check four kinds of standing separately: location / disclosure, reuse / representation; judge translation by its task: local sufficiency can coexist with nonalignment; check bridging item by item across periods, multiple follow-ups ≠ multiple independent trials, significance labels ≠ a test of differences; bounded improvements can hold while agendas, refusal, and remedies require separate judgments; no overall civilizational ranking. Drawn by the author based on D.12; navigation, surveys, and test values are teaching constructions, and no restricted knowledge was used.","caption":"Drawn by the author based on D.12; navigation, surveys, and test values are teaching constructions, and no restricted knowledge was used."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented, and the following references to selecting, switching, or clicking describe future design ideas only. Switch specific uses, permission-related standing, item bridging, and correlation conditions for follow-ups to show which conclusions can be retained.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"The actual protocol separates provenance, access, and permissions, making it useful for showing differences in standing; existence of a template is not upgraded into actual authorization or universal effectiveness."},"openQuestions":["Not all original studies and community materials for 12.01–12.13 were obtained; no complete civilizational history is reconstructed and no group is represented.","The TK label case checks only official design and explanation, not actual adoption or protective effects in specific communities."],"taskId":"S-D-12","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Lines 4705–4720 of the complete historical Chinese research manuscript; see sourceRefs for each section's precise range","claim_support":["D.12: terms, contracts, burdens of argument, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"localcontexts-labels","title":"TK Labels","url":"https://localcontexts.org/labels/traditional-knowledge-labels/","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"The page lists no publication date","locator":"Paragraphs 2–3 of the introduction; explanations of the three categories; Permission Labels lines 53–54","claim_support":["Provenance, access protocols, and permissions are listed separately; other uses require direct contact with the relevant cultural authority"],"limits":["Establishes only the design and explanation of rules, not actual authorization or implementation outcomes in all communities."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"localcontexts-community","title":"TK Community Use Only","url":"https://localcontexts.org/label/tk-community-use-only/","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"The page lists no publication date","locator":"Both paragraphs of Why use this Label; both paragraphs of Template Text; opening paragraph of How to use (lines 9–17)","claim_support":["Conditions of circulation normally within the community, and selection and customization of labels by the community"],"limits":["No restricted material was used, and no specific authorization or implementation outcome is judged."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"D.12　宗教、文明比较与关系性知识; Chinese source lines 5158–5173; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-12/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-12/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-12/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-12/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text and section locations correspond to v2; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"5778b7118ad0dc2fc58637955015857f244a652634cc14273ced37f5b33588b3","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-12.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-12.md"}`),c={name:"en/theory/appendix-d/d-12.md"};function d(u,e,h,m,p,f){const i=n("SectionLesson");return a(),s("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=o("",1)),r(i,{section:"D.12"}),e[1]||(e[1]=l("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const b=t(c,[["render",d]]);export{y as __pageData,b as default};
